#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# hankel_transform

#% text
# `hankel_transform(f, r, s)` gives the Hankel transform of order 0, `integrate(f bessel_j(0, s r) r, [r, 0, inf])`; `hankel_transform(f, r, s, nu)` the transform of order nu.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Closed forms for sums of `c r^mu`, `c r^mu exp(-a r)`, `c r^mu exp(-a r^2)`, `c r^nu (r^2 + a^2)^-p` and `g(r) dirac_delta(r - b)` (DLMF 10.22; Erdelyi et al., Tables of Integral Transforms II). Other functions leave the transform unevaluated.

#% item
# A numeric s evaluates the closed form at s.

#% item
# Functions without a classical transform (`cos(a r)`, `sin(a r)`, `sin_integral(r^2)`) get the generalized (Abel) transform, the limit as e -> 0+ of the transform of `exp(-e r) f(r)`.

#% item
# `cos(a r)/(r^2 + b^2)` and `sin(a r)/(r^2 + b^2)` have a closed form only for s >= a resp. s <= a (both integrals converge at s = a); the value carries that range as `conditional_expression`, and a numeric s outside it leaves the transform unevaluated.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
hankel_transform(exp(-r^2), r, s)
#% output
#= (1/2)*exp((-1/4)*s^2)

#% cell
hankel_transform(1/sqrt(1 + r^2), r, s)
#% output
#= exp(-s)/s

#% cell
hankel_transform(1/r^3, r, s)
#% output
#= -s

#% cell
hankel_transform(r^2*dirac_delta(r - 2), r, s)
#% output
#= 8*bessel_j(0, 2*s)

#% subsection
# Scope

#% cell
hankel_transform(cos(r), r, s)
#% output
#= -heaviside_theta(1 - s)/(1 - s^2)^(3/2)

#% cell
hankel_transform(cos(r)/(1 + r^2), r, s)
#% output
#= conditional_expression(bessel_k(0, s)*cosh(1), s >= 1)

#% cell
hankel_transform(sin_integral(r^2), r, s)
#% output
#= -2*sin((1/4)*s^2)/s^2

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `inverse_hankel_transform`, `bessel_j`, `fourier_transform`
